     "title": "When an EI Test Failed to Predict Anything",
     "content": "A mid-sized financial services firm, aiming to become more 'emotionally intelligent' in its promotion decisions, added a popular self-report EI assessment to its leadership pipeline, alongside the existing personality inventory it already used for hiring. Candidates who scored high on the EI measure were fast-tracked into people-manager roles on the assumption that a high EI score meant a more resonant, effective leader.\n\nEighteen months later, an internal review compared promoted managers' EI scores against actual outcomes: team turnover, 360-degree leadership ratings, and business unit performance. The EI scores showed only a weak relationship with these outcomes - and once the firm's analysts controlled for the personality inventory scores the candidates had already taken, the EI test's added predictive power nearly disappeared. Candidates who scored high on agreeableness and low on neuroticism tended to score high on the EI measure too, and it was hard to tell whether the EI test was measuring something distinct or simply re-measuring personality traits the firm already had data on.\n\nThis is precisely the pattern the construct-overlap critique predicts: a mixed-model EI instrument that feels intuitively meaningful and face-valid, but that adds little statistical value once you've already accounted for standard personality dimensions. The firm didn't conclude that emotional skill was irrelevant to leadership - the 360 data still showed real behavioral differences among managers. They concluded that a self-report EI score, used alone, was the wrong tool for detecting those differences.",
